Chapter: RiskWasm
  cites Games chapter Risk
  cites Games chapter Rng
  cites Foreword chapter List

 The browser interface for Risk, on the handle contract in
 games-backlog.md GAME-11. Twelve territories, their armies, and the player
 table are past an i32, so the page holds the state's address.

 `risk-set-terr` and `risk-recount-players` both write with `list-set-at`,
 which mutates in place, so `rk-wasm-turn` copies the state first.

 We say:

Section: Copying

  rk-copy-terrs : List RiskTerritory, Integer, List RiskTerritory -> List RiskTerritory
  rk-copy-terrs (src) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 12 then acc
    else let t = list-at src i
    in rk-copy-terrs src (i + 1) (list-push acc (RiskTerritory { owner = t.owner, armies = t.armies }))

  rk-copy-players : List RiskPlayer, Integer, Integer, List RiskPlayer -> List RiskPlayer
  rk-copy-players (src) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let p = list-at src i
    in rk-copy-players src n (i + 1) (list-push acc (RiskPlayer { id = p.id, alive = p.alive, total-armies = p.total-armies }))

  rk-copy-state : RiskState -> RiskState
  rk-copy-state (s) =
    RiskState {
      territories = rk-copy-terrs (s.territories) 0 [],
      players = rk-copy-players (s.players) (s.num-players) 0 [],
      current-player = s.current-player,
      turn-number = s.turn-number,
      game-over = s.game-over,
      winner = s.winner,
      num-players = s.num-players,
      phase = s.phase,
      to-place = s.to-place,
      attacks-left = s.attacks-left
    }

Section: The Browser Interface

 A fresh board has nobody's turn open on it, so this opens the first one:
 until `risk-open-turn` runs there is nothing to place and nothing to
 attack with, and a page handed that state would have no move to offer.

  rk-wasm-new : Integer, Integer -> RiskState
  rk-wasm-new (seed) (players) = risk-open-turn (risk-init seed players)

  rk-wasm-owner : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-owner (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= 12 then (-1)
    else let t = list-at (s.territories) i
    in t.owner

  rk-wasm-armies : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-armies (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= 12 then (-1)
    else let t = list-at (s.territories) i
    in t.armies

  rk-wasm-current : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-current (s) = s.current-player

  rk-wasm-turn-number : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-turn-number (s) = s.turn-number

  rk-wasm-done : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-done (s) = if s.game-over then 1 else 0

  rk-wasm-winner : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-winner (s) = s.winner

  rk-wasm-players : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-players (s) = s.num-players

  rk-wasm-alive : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-alive (s) (p) =
    if p < 0 then (-1)
    else if p >= s.num-players then (-1)
    else let pl = list-at (s.players) p
    in if pl.alive then 1 else 0

  rk-wasm-total : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-total (s) (p) =
    if p < 0 then (-1)
    else if p >= s.num-players then (-1)
    else let pl = list-at (s.players) p
    in pl.total-armies

  rk-wasm-reinf : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-reinf (s) (p) =
    if p < 0 then (-1)
    else if p >= s.num-players then (-1)
    else risk-calc-reinforcements s p

  rk-wasm-adjacent : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-adjacent (a) (b) =
    if a < 0 then 0
    else if a >= 12 then 0
    else if b < 0 then 0
    else if b >= 12 then 0
    else if risk-adjacent a b then 1 else 0

  rk-wasm-continent : RiskState, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-continent (s) (player) (continent) =
    if risk-owns-continent s player continent then 1 else 0

 `risk-do-turn` leaves the next player advanced but not OPENED, because
 the watch-only runner opens each turn as it takes it. A page holds the
 state between turns, so this opens it here.

 A dead player has no turn to open. `risk-do-turn` advances to the next
 seat whether or not it is still alive, because the watch-only runner takes
 the empty turn and advances again on the following call; a page that
 opened one would be handed a board with a reinforcement to place and no
 territory to place it on.

  rk-wasm-turn : RiskState, Integer -> RiskState
  rk-wasm-turn (s) (seed) =
    if s.game-over then s
    else rk-open-live (risk-do-turn (rk-copy-state s) (rng-new seed))

  rk-open-live : RiskState -> RiskState
  rk-open-live (s) =
    if s.game-over then s
    else risk-open-turn (risk-skip-dead s 0)

Section: Taking A Turn Yourself

 Two phases: put the reinforcement down a territory at a time, then take
 up to three attacks, or stop early. Every one of these refuses rather
 than being refused silently, so a page can ring what is legal instead of
 letting a visitor find out by clicking.

  rk-wasm-phase : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-phase (s) = s.phase

  rk-wasm-to-place : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-to-place (s) = s.to-place

  rk-wasm-attacks-left : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-attacks-left (s) = s.attacks-left

  rk-wasm-can-place : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-can-place (s) (terr) =
    if risk-can-place s (s.current-player) terr then 1 else 0

 A REFUSAL ANSWERS THE STATE IT WAS GIVEN, unchanged and un-copied. Copying
 first and letting the engine refuse the copy is the same game and a
 different handle, and a page comparing what came back against what it sent
 cannot then tell a refusal from a move: everything it clicks looks
 accepted. So legality is asked before the copy, never after.

  rk-wasm-place : RiskState, Integer -> RiskState
  rk-wasm-place (s) (terr) =
    if risk-can-place s (s.current-player) terr
      then risk-place-one (rk-copy-state s) (s.current-player) terr
    else s

  rk-wasm-can-attack : RiskState, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-can-attack (s) (from) (to) =
    if risk-can-attack s (s.current-player) from to then 1 else 0

 The dice are the caller's to supply, the same way the watch-only turn
 takes its own: a page that reused one seed would fight the same battle
 every time.

  rk-wasm-attack : RiskState, Integer, Integer, Integer -> RiskState
  rk-wasm-attack (s) (from) (to) (seed) =
    if risk-can-attack s (s.current-player) from to
      then risk-attack (rk-copy-state s) (s.current-player) from to (rng-new seed)
    else s

  rk-wasm-can-stop : RiskState -> Integer
  rk-wasm-can-stop (s) =
    if risk-can-stop s (s.current-player) then 1 else 0

  rk-wasm-stop : RiskState -> RiskState
  rk-wasm-stop (s) =
    if risk-can-stop s (s.current-player)
      then risk-stop (rk-copy-state s) (s.current-player)
    else s

 Whether a territory of yours has anything worth attacking next to it,
 which is what a page rings when you are choosing what to attack FROM.

  rk-wasm-can-attack-from : RiskState, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-can-attack-from (s) (terr) =
    if s.phase /= 1 then 0
    else if terr < 0 then 0
    else if terr >= 12 then 0
    else rk-any-target s terr 0

  rk-any-target : RiskState, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rk-any-target (s) (from) (i) =
    if i >= 12 then 0
    else if risk-can-attack s (s.current-player) from i then 1
    else rk-any-target s from (i + 1)

Section: The Whole Game

  rk-wasm-run : Integer, Integer -> RiskResult
  rk-wasm-run (seed) (players) = run-risk-game seed players

  rk-wasm-res-winner : RiskResult -> Integer
  rk-wasm-res-winner (r) = r.winner

  rk-wasm-res-turns : RiskResult -> Integer
  rk-wasm-res-turns (r) = r.turns

  rk-wasm-res-owner : RiskResult, Integer -> Integer
  rk-wasm-res-owner (r) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (r.territories) then (-1)
    else list-at (r.territories) i

